  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nCurrent 500mb analysis plots an upper low swinging through northern \nMexico this afternoon, with a ridge axis extending from central \nCA into the Great Basin. This is providing northerly flow aloft, \nwith the surface high placed in the Great Basin. Surface pressure \ngradients look to have peaked earlier this morning, with -10.7 mb \nSAN-TPH at 15z (7AM) and -4.6 mb SAN-DAG at 17z (9AM), and are now\nbeginning to relax. The offshore winds also look to have peaked \ncompared to earlier this morning, but will still remain breezy \ninto this afternoon. North-northeast gusts 15-20 mph across the \ninland valleys will continue this afternoon, with gusts up to \n35-40+ mph within mountain passes and adjacent foothills. \nTemperatures are quite mild as a result of the offshore flow, with\nmany coastal/valley locations in the mid to upper 80s, about \n10-15 degrees above seasonal averages. As the upper level pattern \nprogresses and the surface high slides southeastward toward the \nFour Corners into Sunday, the offshore pressure gradient will \ncontinue to gradually weaken and offshore winds turn more \neasterly. Stronger wind gusts will favor east-west passes in \nRiverside/San Diego counties as a result, with top gusts 30-40 mph\nin the mountains/passes. Occasional 15-20 mph gusts may stretch \ninto the foothills and inland valleys Sunday afternoon. Persistent\noffshore flow will keep the temperatures unseasonably warm, with \nsimilar high temperatures to today, perhaps even a few degrees \nwarmer. While offshore flow looks to stay in place into Monday, \nthe weakening trend will continue, with elevated breezes (near \n20-25 mph) remain restricted to the mountains/passes. The heat \nwill stick around though, with highs on Monday being a few degrees\ncooler than Sunday.\n\nTuesday into the end of next week...\n\nCome midweek, global ensembles are consistent with an upper ridge \nspreading into south-central California and the Southwest. This \nkeeps our weather relatively quiet, with the main story being the \npersistence of mild conditions, high temperatures on Tuesday-\nWednesday stay in the low to mid 80s for the coasts/valleys, \nmaintaining the 10-15 degree departure from average. \n\nGlobal ensemble cluster then feature an upper low to follow the \nridge some time late week, but clusters diverge on the progression \nof this low. The clusters favor a pretty far south storm, with the \nGFS cluster being too far south to bring precipitation chances, and \nthe Euro cluster being slightly less south, bringing slight \nprecipitation chances. Timeframe would be late week, but uncertainty \nin any rainfall occurring remains quite high at this time. For \nnow, rainfall chances would increase the further south you go.",
